README

This is the closest we'll come to release notes with this
distribution, so read carefully.  I'll use this space to
document some standard conventions I follow.  Please, do
not take this to be Stepstone's official style guide.  This
is just one person's attempt to pass along knowledge on one
way of doing things that you might find helpful.

This software builds one version of the interlocked wheel
simulation described in the article "Pictorial Design
Notation for Software-ICs" from the July/August '90 issue
of JOOP.  Since there was a significant amount of time
between the work being done and the paper being published,
the source as it appears in this distribution is slightly
different than the outlines in the article.  In particular,
I've opted to (a) rely on the plotting facilities available in
Sun OS4.n, and (b) distribute source that better reflects 
how I would implement a code generator for the diagrams in
the article.

To use the demo application on a Sun character display:

  simDemo 100 50 | plot -Tcrt

The following are a set of general comments on the code 
contained in the distribution:

1. Interface files are created from completed implementation
   files.  Therefor, the interface files will seem rather
   bland.  In a commercial release situation, this would be
   unacceptable.

2. Andy's Rule of "#imports" - Import the interface for any
   class that's directly referenced in the implementation
   file.  This means redundant references to the same file.
   That's OK, since the import preprocessor command will
   only include a file once per compilation.  It's my own
   preference to over-document to decrease the amount of
   information the programmer has to remember while looking
   at a class.

3. Always include the superclass and instance variables in
   the implementation file.  While not strictly required 
   (it is required in the class's interface), it helps (see
   #2 above).

4. While the statement "self = [self new]" is seen in the
   original source code published in JOOP, it really should
   be avoided.  The "+create" method in BBObject.m shows a
   better way of setting up new instances of classes.

5. The comments in the code will refer to producers and
   consumers of components.  The producer is the party that
   is responsible for the design, development, test, and
   packaging of the reusable component.  The consumer is
   the party that uses the component as is.

6. Classes that need access to the interface for Object will
   import the interface for BBObject.  BBObject uses the 
   poseAs mechanism to add behavior to the predefined Object
   class.  [For NeXT users: I'm not sure how well poseAs is
   supported in NeXT's version of the Objective-C compiler.
   You might have to convert BBObject from a separate class
   that uses posing, to a category of class Object.]

7. The two instance methods, "circumference:" and "offset:" 
   in the GearModel class have been merged into one method,
   called "circumference:offset:".

8. The 'initialize' method in the clock class sets the
   period instance var to zero.  Although this is redundant
   (instance vars are automatically set to zero when
   allocated), it helps to document all assumptions.

9. Several of the classes (Clock, GearModel, etc.) have
   dummy methods defined.  These methods provide the type
   information the Objective-C compiler needs to translate
   the parameters and return type for the output messages
   sent by these components.  

10. Small error in paper.  Figure 3.3 in the paper should 
    show "radius:" as "circumference:offset:".

I hope this code helps.  Please remember that this is being 
sent as unsupported.  That doesn't mean I don't want to start
discussions on alternative ways of implementing classes, it
means I'm not very interested in helping to port the code to
other platforms.  I look forward to any comments you might
have.

Andy N.
